WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.949
Good morning John! I'm rotating through my
posters. That's right. I'm on my wall.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:08.090
So a couple of weeks ago I introduced you
to some of the people of Savann Tabak, a village

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:12.800
that I visited while I was in Haiti. Genna
and Ulna and Isaiid and Botes and Jan Baptiste.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.249
So right after we first met the people of
Savann Tabak, they decided to take us on a

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:20.640
walk to where they are currently getting their
water. Now the first thing that we see on

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.939
this walk is the broken well, the well that
was built at some point and broke who knows

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:31.029
how long ago. Several of the older members
of Savann Tabak had a great time arguing about

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:35.060
when the well last worked. There was no consensus,
but there was a great deal of arguing, which

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.210
is, I guess, one of the ways that you pass
the time while you're walking to the well.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:43.170
So we were walking, and, um, walking some
more, and then we were still walking, and

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.860
then we got to the sugar cane mill. So here's
what happens. They take the sugar cane, and

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.030
they run it through a mill that basically
squishes the sugar cane and squeezes all the

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.560
cane juice out. The cane juice that has been
squeezed out of the sugar cane is then placed

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.290
in giant vats, boiled with fires fueled by
the spent stalks of sugar cane, and then it

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.990
becomes a thick brown syrup, and that thick
brown syrup is then put into barrels And then

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.989
it's shipped off, and they ferment it into
rum, which is then sold in Port au Prince.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:09.380
So after our tour of the sugar cane processing
plant, we walked a little more, and then we

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.509
kept walking. And then we walked some more,
and I kept seeing these houses on stilts,

00:01:12.509 --> 00:01:16.219
and I had to ask about that. And I was told
that in fact people don't live up there; uh,

00:01:16.219 --> 00:01:19.859
food is kept up there, and the stilts are
so they can block rodents.

00:01:19.859 --> 00:01:23.210
It's basically the exact same idea as a squirrel-proof
bird feeder, except that instead of one pole,

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.340
there's four poles, and instead of bird food,
it's human food. And instead of squirrels,

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.340
it's rats.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.780
And then we walked some more, and we kept
walking, and we were walking when someone

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.659
gave me some sugar cane. And it was really
good; it tasted like honey, but wetter, and

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:39.009
also different from that.

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:43.219
I took our guide's lead, and I broke mine
into three pieces, and I gave two pieces to

00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:47.049
little kids. And then my first thought was
"But should those kids be having so much sugar?",

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:50.319
and then I immediately felt like a douche.
Among the many problems I encountered in Haiti,

00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:54.319
childhood obesity, not one of them. Isaiid
said that my teeth must be rotten like his

00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:58.462
are if I didn't want all the sugar cane for
myself, but Genna defended me and said that

00:01:58.462 --> 00:01:59.462
I was just a nice guy.

00:01:59.462 --> 00:02:03.109
And we walked some more, and then I stopped
to admire the cactus fence because everything

00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:07.929
in central Haiti is fenced by cactuses. And
then we walked some more, and then we got

00:02:07.929 --> 00:02:15.690
to the spring, and this is not what I pictured
when they said spring; this is a muddy hole

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:20.090
in the ground. Five minutes ago, I was worrying
whether these kids were eating too much sugar,

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.360
and now I'm looking at the warm, muddy hole
in the ground where they get their water.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.000
As we were walking back, the translator was
talking to Jan Baptiste, and I could tell

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.220
that something had gone wrong in the conversation,
and I didn't know what, and I didn't ask at

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.800
the time, but later, I asked our translator
what had happened. She was asking Jan Baptiste

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.780
what the village had done for Carnival this
year, and Carnival is like the biggest, funnest

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.400
time in Haiti. And Jan Baptiste said that,
uh, they had done nothing this year for Carnival,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.570
and our translator wouldn't believe her; she
said "Of course you must have done something

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.370
fun for Carnival, just a little something."
And Jan Baptiste was a voiding the subject,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.450
and finally she just came out and decided
to tell her that right around that time, about

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.980
six people, in a village of sixty households,
had died of cholera.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.400
Cholera is not a nice disease, and if you
have it and don't get to a doctor or a hospital

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.400
within a couple of days, there's a really
good chance that you die. People get cholera

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.620
from drinking dirty water. Dirty water like
the water I saw those women getting for their

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.680
children that day. What they need is a well
and an infrastructure within their community

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.850
to help that well be a sustainable source
of water for the lifetime of that community.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.960
And that's what Water.org is going to get
for them.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:31.480
If you go to my.water.org/vlogbrothers, you
will see the progress of Savann Tabak on the

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.210
path to getting that sustainable water source,
and you can give Water.org money so that they

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:39.100
can help the people of Savann Tabak and other
people in similar situations throughout the

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.100
world.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.850
I'm going to make one more video about Haiti,
and in that video, I'm going to talk about

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:47.290
how Water.org and their member organizations
are different and how they are changing the

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:51.710
entire model of charity from the one where
you create people who are dependent on charity

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.990
into one where you create people who become
independent.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:58.180
I hope that you are looking forward to that
video; I am looking forward to making it.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.300
John, I'll see you on Wednesday.

